\title{\bfseries The Arithmetic of the Anti-Fibonacci Sequence}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{17 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study the \emph{anti-Fibonacci sequence} $a_0 = 1$, $a_{n+1} = a_n + n$, the
addition-forgetting counterpart of the Fibonacci recurrence in which the second summand
is replaced by the step index. In place of exponential growth and the golden ratio, the
sequence grows quadratically, $a_n = \tfrac12 n(n-1) + 1$, and its consecutive ratio tends
to $1$. We show that essentially all of its arithmetic is governed by the single identity
$8a_n = (2n-1)^2 + 7$, which transports every question about the sequence into a question
about the binary quadratic form $x^2+7$ of discriminant $-7$. Exploiting this we obtain: a
constant-time counting formula $C(N) = \lfloor(\lfloor\sqrt{8N-7}\rfloor+1)/2\rfloor+1$
with sharp integer bounds, the asymptotic $C(N)\sim\sqrt{2N}$ and natural density zero,
together with a one-square-root membership test; a complete Pell classification of the
square terms, showing there are infinitely many; an exact equivalence between three-term
arithmetic progressions in the sequence and Pythagorean triples with odd hypotenuse, with
an explicit infinite family whose common difference is three times a square-pyramidal
number, contrasted with a rigidity theorem for Fibonacci progressions; a two-squares
criterion for sums of two terms with a non-representable set of lower density at least
$2/9$, and a proof that the sequence is an additive basis of order four; the prime divisor
law ``$p$ divides some term iff $p=7$ or $p\equiv 1,2,4 \pmod 7$'', with both the divisor
set and its complement infinite; a residue spectrum modulo an odd prime $p$ of size exactly
$(p+1)/2$; the minimal period modulo $m$, equal to $m$ for odd $m$ and $2m$ for even $m$;
and the exact law $\gcdd(a_n,a_{n+1}) = 2$ iff $n \equiv 2 \pmod 4$ and $1$ otherwise.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} anti-Fibonacci sequence, lazy caterer numbers, Pell equation,
Pythagorean triples, quadratic reciprocity, additive basis, natural density, Pisano period.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}

The Fibonacci recurrence $F_{n+1} = F_n + F_{n-1}$ is the archetype of self-reference in
arithmetic. Its characteristic equation $t^2 = t+1$ has dominant root the golden ratio
$\varphi = (1+\sqrt5)/2$, whence $F_{n+1}/F_n \to \varphi$. The presence of a quadratic
irrationality in the growth constant is not incidental: it is why the sequence's arithmetic
is hard. Determining which Fibonacci numbers are squares (only $0,1,144$) required a
genuinely deep argument; determining which are prime remains open.

We investigate what happens when the recurrence's \emph{memory} is weakened in the mildest
possible way: keep the shape ``next term $=$ current term $+$ something'', but let the
``something'' be the number of steps taken rather than the previous term.

\begin{definition}[Anti-Fibonacci sequence]
The \emph{anti-Fibonacci sequence} $(a_n)_{n\ge0}$ is defined by
\[ a_0 = 1, \qquad a_{n+1} = a_n + n \quad (n \ge 0). \]
\end{definition}

Its first terms are $1, 1, 2, 4, 7, 11, 16, 22, 29, 37, 46, 56, 67, 79, 92, \ldots$

\begin{proposition}[Closed form]\label{prop:closed}
For all $n \ge 0$,
\[ 2a_n + n = n^2 + 2, \qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad a_n = \frac{n(n-1)}{2} + 1 .\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Induction. For $n=0$ both sides are $2$. If $2a_n + n = n^2+2$, then
$2a_{n+1} + (n+1) = 2a_n + 2n + n + 1 = (n^2+2)+2n+1 = (n+1)^2+2$.
\end{proof}

We record the closed form in the subtraction-free shape $2a_n + n = n^2+2$: every argument
below can then be run inside the nonnegative integers.

Two consequences separate the sequence from its namesake. First, $a_n \sim n^2/2$: growth
is quadratic. Second,
\[ \frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n} = 1 + \frac{2n}{n^2-n+2} \longrightarrow 1, \]
so the consecutive ratio converges to $1$, not to $\varphi$. The golden ratio is absent in
every asymptotic sense.

\subsection{A geometric incarnation}

The numbers $a_n = \binom n2 + 1$ are the \emph{central polygonal numbers}, better known as
the \emph{lazy caterer's sequence}: $a_n$ is the maximum number of regions into which $n-1$
straight lines divide the plane. Adding an $n$-th line in general position meets each of the
previous $n-1$ lines once, so it is cut into $n$ pieces and creates $n$ new regions --- exactly
the recurrence $a_{n+1} = a_n + n$.

\subsection{The master identity}

\begin{proposition}[Master identity]\label{prop:master}
For all $n \ge 0$ we have $8a_n = (2n-1)^2 + 7$ over $\Z$; equivalently
$8a_{p+1} = (2p+1)^2 + 7$ for $p \ge 0$, an identity in $\N$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$8a_n = 4n^2 - 4n + 8 = (2n-1)^2 + 7$.
\end{proof}

Every result below is a corollary of Proposition~\ref{prop:master} combined with a classical
theorem about the form $x^2+7$. The discriminant $-7$ places us in the imaginary quadratic
field $\Q(\sqrt{-7})$, whose ring of integers has class number one; this is the structural
reason the answers are clean congruence conditions.

